"<div><div class=\" response-message\"></div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h2>Payoff Matrix</h2>\n<p>✅ Matrix constructed</p>\n<p>This scenario is the classic <strong>Prisoner's Dilemma</strong>, a fundamental model in game theory that demonstrates why two completely rational individuals might not cooperate, even if it appears in their best interest to do so.</p>\n<h3>1. Game Structure Analysis</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Game Type</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-cooperative</strong>: Players make decisions independently without the ability to form binding contracts.</li>\n<li><strong>Simultaneous</strong>: Both players choose their strategy at the same time without knowing the other's choice.</li>\n<li><strong>One-shot</strong>: The interaction occurs only once, meaning there is no opportunity to build trust or retaliate in future rounds.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-zero-sum</strong>: The total &quot;utility&quot; or years served is not constant; the collective outcome varies based on the choices made (ranging from 2 total years to 10 total years).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Information</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Imperfect Information</strong>: Neither player knows the other's move until after the decision is made.</li>\n<li><strong>Complete Information</strong>: Both players are fully aware of the rules, the strategies available, and the resulting payoffs for both parties.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Strategy Spaces</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Discrete</strong>: Each player has exactly two choices: <strong>Betray</strong> (Defect) or <strong>Silent</strong> (Cooperate).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Symmetry</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Symmetric</strong>: The payoffs for a strategy pair depend only on the strategies themselves, not on which player is playing them. If the players swapped roles, the payoffs would remain identical.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>2. Payoff Matrix</h3>\n<p>In game theory, payoffs are typically represented as <strong>utility</strong>. Since prison time is a negative outcome, the payoffs are represented as negative numbers (where -1 is &quot;better&quot; than -10).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th align=\"left\">Suspect A \\ Suspect B</th><th align=\"left\"><strong>Silent</strong> (Cooperate)</th><th align=\"left\"><strong>Betray</strong> (Defect)</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Silent</strong> (Cooperate)</td><td align=\"left\">(-1, -1)</td><td align=\"left\">(-10, 0)</td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Betray</strong> (Defect)</td><td align=\"left\">(0, -10)</td><td align=\"left\">(-5, -5)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>(Payoff format: Suspect A, Suspect B)</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h3>3. Detailed Outcome Analysis</h3>\n<h4>Outcome: (Silent, Silent)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Outcome for Players</strong>: Both serve 1 year.</li>\n<li><strong>Numerical Payoff</strong>: (-1, -1)</li>\n<li><strong>Explanation</strong>: This is the <strong>Socially Optimal</strong> outcome. By cooperating with each other (remaining silent), they minimize their total collective time in prison (2 years total). However, this state is unstable because each player has an individual incentive to cheat.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Outcome: (Betray, Silent)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Outcome for Players</strong>: Suspect A is set free; Suspect B serves 10 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Numerical Payoff</strong>: (0, -10)</li>\n<li><strong>Explanation</strong>: Suspect A &quot;free-rides&quot; on Suspect B’s cooperation. A receives the highest possible individual payoff (0 years) by betraying B, while B receives the &quot;Sucker’s Payoff&quot; (the worst possible outcome).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Outcome: (Silent, Betray)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Outcome for Players</strong>: Suspect A serves 10 years; Suspect B is set free.</li>\n<li><strong>Numerical Payoff</strong>: (-10, 0)</li>\n<li><strong>Explanation</strong>: The inverse of the previous scenario. Suspect B acts rationally in their own self-interest to avoid prison, leaving Suspect A to face the maximum penalty for remaining loyal.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Outcome: (Betray, Betray)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Outcome for Players</strong>: Both serve 5 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Numerical Payoff</strong>: (-5, -5)</li>\n<li><strong>Explanation</strong>: This is the <strong>Nash Equilibrium</strong>.\n<ul>\n<li>If A thinks B will stay silent, A should betray (0 is better than -1).</li>\n<li>If A thinks B will betray, A should also betray (-5 is better than -10).</li>\n<li>Since &quot;Betray&quot; is the <strong>Dominant Strategy</strong> for both players, they both choose it. Even though (-1, -1) is better for both, they end up at (-5, -5) because neither can trust the other to remain silent in a non-cooperative, one-shot environment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div></div><div><div class=\" completion-message response-message\"></div></div>"